dramratebench: no physical DRAM core fronts two banks on this part, so the
  `samecore` arm (same inbound NoC link, different GDDR6 channel) is SKIPPED.
  Without it, `onechan` cannot separate a channel limit from the limit of the
  one router link every reader's flow converges on. Say so when reporting.

dramratebench: wrote /home/nx09/nx09/nebriscv/tt_traces/dram-write-session/run1.csv (0 failures)
dramratebench: 40 point(s); 36 of 36 multi-reader point(s) had a reader wait at the
  barrier; 0 point(s) had a reader that did not verify its DRAM tag

dramratebench: SUSTAINED RATE -- N readers, 1048576 B each, ONE channel
  readers   aggregate B/cycle   per reader   aggregate GB/s   fanchan B/cycle
        1              46.278       46.278          62.48           46.368
        2              46.142       23.071          62.29           92.581
        4              46.408       11.602          62.65          145.257
        8              45.671        5.709          61.66          194.235
       12              46.196        3.850          62.36          173.577
       16              46.623        2.914          62.94          210.610
       24              47.076        1.961          63.55          204.707
       32              47.134        1.473          63.63          235.354
       48              47.144        0.982          63.64          194.230
      120              47.159        0.393          63.66          227.610
  No published table applies: wh_dram#performance is a WORMHOLE page, and this
  part is blackhole. Blackhole publishes no DRAM tile page at all -- no per-channel
  rate, no address map -- so there is nothing here to compare against and
  nothing measured here can supply it.
  fanchan aggregate 46.368 -> 227.610 B/cycle over 1 -> 120 readers (x4.91)
  onechan aggregate 46.295 -> 47.139 B/cycle over 1 -> 120 readers (x1.02)
  VERDICT: ENDPOINT BOUND -- the fan-out control scaled x4.91 while the
  same readers on ONE channel managed only x1.02, which is 21% of it.
  Same reader count, same issue loop, same transaction size: only the
  endpoint differed, so the endpoint is what cost the difference. This is
  the shape tt-sim's DramChannels term asserts. It is CORROBORATION and
  never provenance: it cannot make dram.bandwidth chargeable, least of all
  on a part with no published DRAM page.
  samecore: not run on this part, so a channel limit and the limit of the
  one router link every onechan flow converges on stay confounded.

